{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`LLMConfig` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:473`) exposes `base_url` and `api_key` but no way to\nattach request headers, so an operator behind a gateway cannot label rebar's gate calls. A raw\ndict is unsafe as-is: both vendor SDKs merge caller headers last, so a caller-supplied\n`Authorization` silently displaces the real credential. This ticket adds the configuration\nsurface and the validation that makes it safe; nothing is sent yet.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `headers: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)` to `LLMConfig`, resolved in\n`from_env` by the three-layer precedence `mcp_servers` already uses at\n`src/rebar/llm/config.py:574-594` (CLI `-c llm.headers=<json>` > `REBAR_LLM_HEADERS` JSON >\nthe `[tool.rebar.llm]` file table), so there is one documented precedence story.\n\n**Precedence only — deliberately not error handling.** `mcp_servers` degrades malformed JSON\nto `{}` silently in all three layers (`config.py:582-583` for env/CLI, `:593-594` for the file\ntable). `headers` does not copy that: malformed JSON raises `LLMConfigError` naming the layer,\nin **every** layer. The file layer accepts either a native TOML table or a JSON string\n(mirroring `config.py:585-592`); a malformed JSON string there, or a value that is neither a\ntable nor a string, or JSON that parses to a non-object, is a hard error rather than a silent\n`{}`. Silently discarding headers would\nleave an operator with an unattributed gateway and no signal, contradicting the fail-loud rule\nbelow. One fail-loud story for the whole surface.\n\nGrammar and validation live in a new leaf module `src/rebar/llm/headers.py`, testable without\nbuilding a config.\n\n**The `${...}` grammar is closed.** Exactly two namespaces, `env` and `run`; `headers.py` owns\nthat set and the `${run:...}` key vocabulary (`trace_id`, `ticket_id`, `operation`) as a module\nconstant, so the grammar is self-contained and does not depend on the ticket supplying those\nvalues. Any `${` begins a placeholder; a literal `${` is written `$${` (the `$$` escape, the\nconvention OpenTelemetry adopted after its own users hit this). Dispositions at config-resolution time:\n\n- `${env:VAR}` — resolved now; unset is a hard error, matching the `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`\n  rule that a missing path never silently degrades (`config.py:19-21`).\n- `${run:KEY}` — left unresolved (its value exists only inside a gate run), but `KEY` is\n  checked against the closed vocabulary now, so a typo fails at configuration time.\n- Unrecognized namespace (`${foo:bar}`), missing colon (`${envVAR}`), or unclosed `${...}` —\n  hard `LLMConfigError`, never a silently-accepted literal. Passing placeholder text through as\n  a header value would be a silent misconfiguration in a feature meant for attribution.\n\n**Ordering: substitute first, then validate.** Every check runs on the resolved value, never on\na literal containing `${env:...}`. Validating first would let an env var whose value holds\nCR/LF carry an injected header past the check meant to stop it — a configured\n`${env:GATEWAY_TAG}` where `GATEWAY_TAG` is `foo\\r\\nInjected: bar` passes a check applied to\nthe literal, and substitution then injects. Validation therefore runs after `${env:...}`\nsubstitution with any `${run:...}` placeholder still present as literal text (which holds no\ncontrol characters), and the delivery ticket re-runs it after substituting `${run:...}`.\n\nRejected with a named `LLMConfigError`: header names in the denied set `authorization`,\n`x-api-key`, `cookie`, `host`, `proxy-authorization` (case-insensitive, exact-name — these\nforge or displace authentication; the error names `llm.api_key` as the supported channel, so the\ndenylist redirects to a first-class field rather than merely forbidding — the posture Prometheus\ntakes, whose `ReservedHeaders` denylist is legitimate because it ships dedicated auth blocks); names outside RFC 7230 token characters; values containing\nCR, LF or NUL. A denylist rather than an allowlist because arbitrary gateway header names are\nthe point of the feature, and the threats being closed — credential displacement and protocol\ninjection — are themselves closed sets.\n\nDocumentation for this surface is delivered by sibling `98b6-efa8-bf15-4760`, per the epic's\ndecomposition, to keep child responsibilities non-overlapping.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the `headers` field and its three-layer resolution in `from_env`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/headers.py` (new) — grammar, `${env:...}` resolution, name/value validation.\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_headers.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real functions — pure string resolution and validation,\nno boundary to cross.\n\n- Happy path: a literal header and an `${env:VAR}` header both resolve.\n- Grammar dispositions: `${foo:bar}`, `${envVAR}`, `${en:VAR}`, `${env:VAR`, `${run:bogus}`\n  each raise; a well-formed `${run:trace_id}` survives unresolved.\n- Failure paths: each denied name; a name with an invalid character; CR, LF and NUL in values;\n  an `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable; malformed JSON in each of the three layers; a\n  file-table value that is neither a table nor a string.\n- Injection-via-indirection: a value of `${env:VAR}` where `VAR` resolves to a string\n  containing a control character is rejected — the ordering regression test.\n- Negative control: `x-authorization-context` accepted, proving exact-name matching.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `LLMConfig.headers` defaults to `{}` so an unconfigured deployment is byte-unchanged;\n      checked by a test asserting it with no relevant env set.\n- [ ] Headers resolve from all three layers with CLI > env > file precedence; checked by a test\n      setting all three and asserting the CLI value wins.\n- [ ] A `${env:VAR}` value resolves from the environment; checked by a monkeypatched test.\n- [ ] An `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable raises `LLMConfigError` naming the variable;\n      checked by `pytest.raises` asserting the name appears in the message.\n- [ ] An unrecognized namespace, missing colon, unclosed placeholder, or `${run:...}` key\n      outside the closed vocabulary each raise `LLMConfigError` rather than being accepted as a\n      literal; checked by a parametrized test over the five forms above.\n- [ ] A well-formed `${run:trace_id}` survives config resolution unresolved; checked by a test\n      asserting the stored value still contains the placeholder.\n- [ ] A value clean as a literal but resolving through `${env:VAR}` to a string containing CR,\n      LF or NUL is rejected; checked by a parametrized test over the three characters, proving\n      validation runs on the resolved value.\n- [ ] Each denied name is rejected case-insensitively; checked by a parametrized test over the\n      five names in three casings.\n- [ ] Malformed JSON raises `LLMConfigError` naming the layer rather than degrading to `{}`,\n      in all three layers; checked by a parametrized test over env, CLI and the file table.\n- [ ] A file-table value that is neither a table nor a string, or a JSON string parsing to a\n      non-object, raises `LLMConfigError`; checked by a parametrized test over both shapes.\n- [ ] No gateway-vendor header name appears as a string constant in shipped code under\n      `src/rebar/llm/`; checked by a test scanning that tree — excluding its own file — for the\n      case-insensitive literals `x-litellm-`, `langfuse_` and `helicone-`, so the portability\n      guarantee is enforced rather than merely stated.\n- [ ] A literal `${` is expressible as `$${` and survives resolution unsubstituted; checked by\n      a test asserting the resolved value contains `${`.\n- [ ] The rejection message for a denied name names `llm.api_key`; checked by asserting that\n      string appears in the raised error.\n- [ ] `x-authorization-context` is accepted; checked by a test asserting it survives."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787719421816720001,"uuid":"a310676a-f516-4b7d-9b5a-d986f5e9deb6"}